an river  ten years ago though line five's legacy  is long and relatively clean  construction of the 90 million dollar  inter-provincial pipeline from edmonton  alberta to superior wisconsin the first  phase of the pipeline to the u.s was  built 70 years ago then in the early 50s  line five was constructed through  michigan to sarnia ontario between  michigan's upper and lower peninsulas  more than six kilometers of that  pipeline crosses the mackinaw straits  it's this sensitive area that most  worries michigan's governor the light  oil and natural gas liquids delivered by  our line five pipeline enbridge says  there's never been a leak here and  proposes to build a tunnel into the  riverbed and bury the pipeline to  protect it from rupturing but michigan  wants to shut off the oil so canada has  invoked a 1977 nation donation treaty  which ottawa says prevents michigan from  interfering with in any way the  transmission of hydrocarbon in transit  it shows how high the stakes are what if  this was shut down in the next two or  three months this would truly be uh  you know chaos dealing with people's  lives in their homes the us has certain  obligations to its uh treaty partner the  canadians it's awkward for president  biden who visited michigan today he  doesn't want to offend canada or  democratic progressives like governor  whitmer but ottawa is forcing  washington's hand by invoking the treaty  canada will ask the federal government  of the united states to  take control of the issue and preempt  michigan the treaty between prime  minister pierre trudeau and president  jimmy carter came during the energy  crisis of the 1970s the americans wanted  less dependence on middle east oil and  trudeau's approval for alaskan gas  through this country 44 years later it  is canada that has turned to the treaty  to ensure its energy can keep flowing  erik sorensen global news toronto  still ahead how the pandemic is raising  the bar for mental health benefits  over the course of the pandemic many  canadians have been struggling with  their mental well-being new polling from  ipsos shows 28 of canadians admit their  mental health has deteriorated as a  result there is more demand for  counseling and support but azan gaviola  reports benefits for workers are often  limited  i was doubting myself a lot didn't feel  like i could meet challenges head-on the  way that i usually could amber smith hit  an emotional wall in february she says  it was taking a toll on her work and her  personal relationships so she tried  something she's never done before she  started seeing a psychologist it's been  you know tough to feel isolated and like  you don't have access to your  friends and family in the way that you  would it was really hard to just feel  like yourself millennials and gen z are  most likely to admit it's a struggle  when you ask them to self-report their  level of mental health they are more  likely to report it as poor or bad  compared to their older counterparts  there's been a huge spike in virtual  therapy sessions at the ontario shores  center for mental health mirroring a  trend seen right across the country we  used to do about  400 virtual care visits a month  pre-pandemic  and  we are now doing anywhere between  4 000 and 6 000 virtual care visits  a month given how competitive the labor  market has become employers who want to  keep and attract the best workers need  to consider offering mental health  benefits if they don't already and  increasing caps the majority of the  plans still provide a fairly modest  amount maybe 500 a year in support for  that type of coverage and so um you know  depending on your personal needs it may  not be enough i also value an employer  though who creates an environment like a  workplace of well-being where i know i  can come to work and i can find support  on those days when i don't feel myself  the pandemic has raised the bar for  mental health support in the workplace  ann gaviola global